<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="trans-lycopene biosynthesis II (oxygenic phototrophs and green sulfur bacteria)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6475" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD1F-114 &quot;&lt;i&gt;All-trans&lt;/i&gt;-lycopene&quot;| is a bright red carotenoid pigment and phytochemical found in many photosynthetic organisms, as well as some non-photosynthetic organisms. Its name is derived from the name of the tomato plant, |FRAME: TAX-4081|, in which it is found in large amounts. |FRAME: CPD1F-114 &quot;&lt;i&gt;All-trans&lt;/i&gt;-lycopene&quot;| is an important intermediate in the biosynthesis of many carotenoids, which are responsible for pigmentation, photosynthesis, and photo-protection in plants, algae, and other organisms. The first committed step of carotenoid biosynthesis is the formation of |FRAME: CPD-12321|. Two molecules of |FRAME: GERANYLGERANYL-PP| (GGPP) are condensed to give |FRAME: CPD-464|, followed by rearrangement of the cyclopropylcarbinyl intermediate to |FRAME: CPD-12321|. This conversion is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.32|. Different strategies exist among different organisms for the conversion of |FRAME: CPD-12321| to |FRAME: CPD1F-114|. In fungi and most non-oxygenic bacteria the pathway proceeds via exclusively &lt;i&gt;all-trans&lt;/i&gt; isomers (see |FRAME: PWY-5942|). In oxygenic phototrophs such as plants, algae and cyanobacteria, as well as green sulfur bacteria, the pathway proceeeds via different &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; isomers, and the final conversion to &lt;i&gt;all-trans&lt;/i&gt; occurs at the very end of the pathway, where |FRAME: CPD-7496| is converted to |FRAME: CPD1F-114| (this pathway). It should be noted that exceptions do occur - for example, the cyanobacterium |FRAME: TAX-251221| utilizes the bacterial pathway |CITS: [15811329]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; In oxygenic phototrophs such as plants, algae and cyanobacteria, as well as in green sulfur bacteria, |FRAME: CPD-12321| undergoes a series of four desaturation reactions leading to the formation of |FRAME:CPD1F-114| &lt;i&gt;via&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CPD-7535|, |FRAME: CPD-7526| and |FRAME: CPD-7496|. To catalyze this transformation, plants and cyanobacteria require two phylogenetically related desaturases [|FRAME: EC-1.3.5.5| (PDS, crtP) and |FRAME: EC-1.3.5.6| (ZDS, crtQ)] and two isomerases [|FRAME: EC-5.2.1.12| (Z-ISO) and |FRAME: EC-5.2.1.13| (CRTISO, crtH)]. The first desaturase, |FRAME: CPLX1F-15| (|FRAME: AT4G14210|), removes two hydrogens and introduces &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt; double bonds at 11 and 11&apos;, along with a &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; bond at the 9 and 9&apos; double bond positions, producing |FRAME: CPD-7535| which is isomerized at the 15-&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; double bond to |FRAME: CPD-7526| by |FRAME: AT1G10830-MONOMER| (|FRAME: AT1G10830|). The isomerization can occur spontaneously in light, but not in the dark. The second desaturase, |FRAME: CPLX1F-19| (|FRAME: AT3G04870|), removes two hydrogens from |FRAME: CPD-7526|, introducing &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; double bonds at the 7 and 7&apos; positions, leading to formation of conjugated &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; double bonds at 7,9 and 7&apos;, 9&apos;. Finally, the second isomerase, |FRAME: MONOMER-12167| (|FRAME: G-9408|), isomerizes the &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; double bonds at 7,9 and 7&apos;, 9&apos;, producing |FRAME: CPD1F-114| |CITS: [18948076][20335404]|. The accumulation of |FRAME: CPD-7496| in &lt;i&gt;tangerine&lt;/i&gt; tomato mutants |CITS: [15557094]|, as well as the biochemical activities tested for CRTISO and ZDS support the existence of this branch of the pathway |CITS: [15557094][18948076]|. It should also be noted that there is no evidence for a Z-ISO gene in the genome of green sulfur bacteria. It is possible that this reaction occurs in these organisms strictly by photoisomerization |CITS: [20335404]|. Plants use oxidized plastoquinones as electron acceptors in the desaturation sequence.</Description>
